Updated targets and instrumentation proposal: For demo performance, suggest ranges rather than hard SLAs — document list load (≈100 docs): ideal ≤1s, demo-acceptable ≤2.5s; real-time edit sync (character/operation-level): ideal ≤250ms, demo-acceptable ≤700ms; presence/avatars visibility: ideal ≤300ms, demo-acceptable ≤1500ms.  
Events to emit (names follow doc_ / collab_ pattern) and key properties: 
- doc_open / doc_create: doc_id, team_id, user_id, timestamp; 
- collab_editor_join / collab_editor_leave: doc_id, team_id, user_id, session_id, timestamp, editor_count; 
- collab_edit_sync: doc_id, team_id, user_id, session_id, timestamp, latency_ms, change_size; 
- collab_comment_create / collab_comment_resolve: doc_id, team_id, user_id, comment_id, parent_comment_id (opt), timestamp; 
- collab_session_end: doc_id, team_id, session_id, session_start_ts, session_end_ts, session_duration_bucket.  
How metrics map: weekly docs with >1 editor = unique doc_id with collab_editor_join events deduped per week; avg co-edit sessions per team = count(collab_session_end) / team; time-to-first-collab = timestamp(first collab_editor_join) − timestamp(doc_create).  
Open questions to resolve before sprint planning: include inline comments for demo (yes/no), do we capture cursor positions in collab_edit_sync (privacy/volume), confirm session_duration_bucket boundaries (suggest 0–5m, 5–30m, 30m+), and any sampling requirements for Data’s pipeline. If this is OK, I’ll update the draft spec, add the instrumentation schema to a ticket, and break the MVP into sprint stories for tomorrow’s planning.
